const t=[{id:"F01",title:"Monsell · 2003 · Task switching",url:"https://psychfiles.net/experimental/Monsell_2003.pdf",type:"research",finding:"Across simple laboratory-task studies, responses after switches were slower and usually more error-prone. Preparation reduced the cost without reliably eliminating it.",limitation:"Narrative review with no pooled workplace-loss estimate. No universal recovery time, productivity percentage, or test of ODA's preparation prompts."},{id:"F02",title:"Albulescu et al. · 2022 · Micro-breaks",url:"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272460",type:"research",finding:"22 samples, 2,335 participants: vigor d = .36 [.16, .55], fatigue improvement .35 [.19, .50], performance .16 [-.04, .37].",limitation:"Outcome samples differ; modest heterogeneous evidence. No optimal duration or activity, reliable demanding-task performance improvement, or proof that all screen leisure fails to restore energy."},{id:"F03",title:"Harkin et al. · 2016 · Monitoring goal progress",url:"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26479070/",type:"research",finding:"138 randomized studies, 19,951 participants overall: monitoring interventions improved goal attainment on average, d = .40 [.32, .48].",limitation:"Varied goals and methods. Moderator associations do not validate a particular diary, require public sharing, or prove this exact focus routine causes gains."},{id:"F04",title:"Gollwitzer & Sheeran · 2006 · Implementation intentions",url:"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2601(06)38002-1",type:"research",finding:"94 tests with 8,461 participants found average goal-attainment support from specific if-then planning, d = .65 [.60, .70].",limitation:"Heterogeneous older studies. No guaranteed effect for a phone-urge sentence or validation of a combined time-blocking package."},{id:"F06",title:"Kushlev, Proulx & Dunn · 2016 · Lower-interruption phone setup",url:"https://interruptions.net/literature/Kushlev-CHI16.pdf",type:"research",finding:"221 students tried two week-long setups. Paired symptom tests used 190 respondents. Alerts off plus phone out of sight reduced self-reported inattention modestly: 2.27 versus 2.38; d = .44.",limitation:"Alerts and visibility/reach changed together. Unblinded self-report; no isolated alert effect, ADHD treatment, or significant direct productivity improvement."},{id:"F07",title:"Mark, Gudith & Klocke · 2008 · Interrupted work, speed, and stress",url:"https://ics.uci.edu/~gmark/chi08-mark.pdf",type:"research",finding:"48 participants in simulated email work used less net task time under interruptions but reported higher stress. Interruption-handling time was subtracted.",limitation:"Small mostly student sample. Not faster total elapsed work, guaranteed unchanged quality, long-term stress effects, or a universal recovery time."},{id:"F08",title:"Biwer et al. · 2023 · Fixed and self-chosen study breaks",url:"https://doi.org/10.1111/bjep.12593",type:"research",finding:"110 students randomized, 87 analyzed in one two-hour session. Fixed 24/6 or 12/3 schedules showed favorable experience patterns; no significant task-completion difference.",limitation:"Attrition and exclusions; longer planned study reduced to one session. No universal best interval, long-term learning gain, or validation of the full branded Pomodoro method."},{id:"F10",title:"Leroy & Glomb · 2018 · Interrupted tasks and return plans",url:"https://ideas.repec.org/a/inm/ororsc/v29y2018i3p380-397.html",type:"research",finding:"Four studies reported that a return plan reduced attention residue and supported decisions or recall on the interrupting task. First field study was a survey.",limitation:"Accessible abstract and university summary only. Exact worksheet, dose, and pooled effects unverified. No demonstrated benefit on the original task after return."},{id:"F11",title:"Lopez et al. · 2018 · Cochrane review of CBT for adult ADHD",url:"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD010840_cognitive-behavioural-therapy-attention-deficit-hyperactivity-disorder-adhd-adults",type:"research",finding:"14 randomized trials with 700 adults assessed structured cognitive-behavioral interventions. Symptom benefits depended on the comparator; certainty ranged from very low to moderate.",limitation:"Clinical programs differ from ODA settings or timers. Little long-term follow-up; this course is not assessment or treatment and gives no medication advice."},{id:"F13",title:"Ruiz Pardo & Minda · 2022 · Preregistered brain-drain replication",url:"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36007374/",type:"research",finding:"A preregistered direct replication of the original second phone-location experiment did not reproduce location differences in the tested tasks.",limitation:"Accessible primary abstract verifies the failed replication but supplies no participant total or exact p value. No proof of exact zero effects everywhere or harmless active checking."},{id:"F14",title:"Hartanto et al. · 2024 · Mere smartphone presence synthesis",url:"https://assets.pubpub.org/mgkf17za/tmb_tmb0000123-41706626995650.pdf",type:"research",finding:"33 studies, 53 samples, 166 effects: no reliable overall impairment from mere phone presence, d = -.02 [-.06, .01]. Working-memory subgroup became nonsignificant after bias correction.",limitation:"Participant totals conflict within the paper, so no definitive total is taught. Task and synthesis differences remain; this does not study active checking or alerts."},{id:"F15",title:"Cal Newport · 2013, updated 2023 · Time-blocking account",url:"https://calnewport.com/deep-habits-the-importance-of-planning-every-minute-of-your-work-day/",type:"technique",finding:"Newport describes a personal scheduling routine with labeled blocks, reactive work, and room to redraw the plan.",limitation:"Practitioner account supplies no intervention trial or effect estimate. It does not establish whether controlled studies of this exact routine exist."},{id:"F16",title:"Parry · 2024 · Brain-drain meta-analysis",url:"https://research.vu.nl/en/publications/does-the-mere-presence-of-a-smartphone-impact-cognitive-performan/",type:"research",finding:"A competing synthesis found an adverse working-memory average while the other cognitive-function summaries were null.",limitation:"Accessible abstract only; exact pooled effects and study-count unit unverified. Heterogeneity and poor power limit conclusions. Mere presence differs from active phone use."},{id:"task-switching",title:"Monsell · 2003 · Task switching",url:"https://psychfiles.net/experimental/Monsell_2003.pdf",type:"research",finding:"Across simple laboratory-task studies, responses after switches were slower and usually more error-prone. Preparation reduced the cost without reliably eliminating it.",limitation:"Narrative review with no pooled workplace-loss estimate. No universal recovery time, productivity percentage, or test of ODA's preparation prompts."},{id:"interruptions",title:"Mark, Gudith & Klocke · 2008 · Interrupted work, speed, and stress",url:"https://ics.uci.edu/~gmark/chi08-mark.pdf",type:"research",finding:"48 participants in simulated email work used less net task time under interruptions but reported higher stress. Interruption-handling time was subtracted.",limitation:"Small mostly student sample. Not faster total elapsed work, guaranteed unchanged quality, long-term stress effects, or a universal recovery time."},{id:"notifications",title:"Kushlev, Proulx & Dunn · 2016 · Lower-interruption phone setup",url:"https://interruptions.net/literature/Kushlev-CHI16.pdf",type:"research",finding:"221 students tried two week-long setups. Paired symptom tests used 190 respondents. Alerts off plus phone out of sight reduced self-reported inattention modestly: 2.27 versus 2.38; d = .44.",limitation:"Alerts and visibility/reach changed together. Unblinded self-report; no isolated alert effect, ADHD treatment, or significant direct productivity improvement."},{id:"phone-presence",title:"Hartanto et al. · 2024 · Mere smartphone presence synthesis",url:"https://assets.pubpub.org/mgkf17za/tmb_tmb0000123-41706626995650.pdf",type:"research",finding:"33 studies, 53 samples, 166 effects: no reliable overall impairment from mere phone presence, d = -.02 [-.06, .01]. Working-memory subgroup became nonsignificant after bias correction.",limitation:"Participant totals conflict within the paper, so no definitive total is taught. Task and synthesis differences remain; this does not study active checking or alerts."},{id:"brain-drain-replication",title:"Ruiz Pardo & Minda · 2022 · Preregistered brain-drain replication",url:"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36007374/",type:"research",finding:"A preregistered direct replication of the original second phone-location experiment did not reproduce location differences in the tested tasks.",limitation:"Accessible primary abstract verifies the failed replication but supplies no participant total or exact p value. No proof of exact zero effects everywhere or harmless active checking."},{id:"microbreaks",title:"Albulescu et al. · 2022 · Micro-breaks",url:"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0272460",type:"research",finding:"22 samples, 2,335 participants: vigor d = .36 [.16, .55], fatigue improvement .35 [.19, .50], performance .16 [-.04, .37].",limitation:"Outcome samples differ; modest heterogeneous evidence. No optimal duration or activity, reliable demanding-task performance improvement, or proof that all screen leisure fails to restore energy."},{id:"focus-ready-to-resume",title:"Leroy & Glomb · 2018 · Interrupted tasks and return plans",url:"https://ideas.repec.org/a/inm/ororsc/v29y2018i3p380-397.html",type:"research",finding:"Four studies reported that a return plan reduced attention residue and supported decisions or recall on the interrupting task. First field study was a survey.",limitation:"Accessible abstract and university summary only. Exact worksheet, dose, and pooled effects unverified. No demonstrated benefit on the original task after return."},{id:"focus-newport-time-blocking",title:"Cal Newport · 2013, updated 2023 · Time-blocking account",url:"https://calnewport.com/deep-habits-the-importance-of-planning-every-minute-of-your-work-day/",type:"technique",finding:"Newport describes a personal scheduling routine with labeled blocks, reactive work, and room to redraw the plan.",limitation:"Practitioner account supplies no intervention trial or effect estimate. It does not establish whether controlled studies of this exact routine exist."},{id:"brain-drain",title:"Ward et al. · 2017 · Original phone-presence experiments",url:"https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/full/10.1086/691462",type:"research",finding:"Original student experiments found small differences on selected working-memory measures under phone-location conditions. Sustained attention did not show the same result.",limitation:"A preregistered direct replication failed. Reviews disagree on working memory and one finds no reliable overall impairment. Do not teach the original result alone as settled fact."},{id:"focus-attention-residue",title:"Leroy · 2009 · Attention residue",url:"https://ideas.repec.org/a/eee/jobhdp/v109y2009i2p168-181.html",type:"research",finding:"Two experiments reported persistent thoughts about a prior task accompanied poorer performance on the subsequent task.",limitation:"Accessible abstract only; detailed methods, sample totals, and effects unavailable. No quantified everyday attention tax or recommendation for chronic time pressure."}],e=[{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"focus-1",courseId:"focus",locale:"en",title:"Busy all day? Protect one useful move",goal:"Do a useful unit of your chosen task and leave a clear return marker.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:10,review:2},direction:{hook:"Still have an unfinished report after an hour of tabs? Give your chosen task an actual turn.",reason:{id:"focus-1-reason",label:"What worthwhile task keeps paying for your optional switches?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["My report stays unfinished","I lose the conversation’s thread","Optional tabs replace my study time","No observed cost yet"]},why:{id:"focus-1-purpose",label:"What would this move make possible in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Choose one task that serves your life, and keep it through this course. In laboratory studies, switching simple tasks slowed the next response and usually increased mistakes. Preparing reduced that cost without reliably removing it. This is not a universal productivity percentage.","For your next small block, produce a useful unit before an optional switch. If a routine interruption needs you, leave a short marker: where you stopped and the first move on return. That marker is a practical adaptation, not proven faster recovery. Skip it when immediate safety or care requires action."],example:{title:"Leila leaves a return marker",text:"After the morning check-in, Leila works on the same budget report for ten minutes. She corrects one travel-cost line before opening email. A colleague needs a figure soon. She writes “Check row 40 next,” handles the request, and returns to that row. The report is unfinished; one correction and one return are real."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your task. New plan? Name an output and cue in this report example.",instructions:["Name the actual task and output before saving this editable example.","At a routine switch, leave a return marker if a brief pause is safe."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Correct one report line; note next step","Write one proposal sentence; note next step","Complete one practice problem; note next step"],minimum:["Correct one report line","Write one usable proposal sentence","Complete one practice problem"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1},{label:"In two days",daysFromToday:2}],cue:["After today’s morning check-in","After lunch, before optional email","After my first available break"],place:["At my work desk","At my kitchen table with task tools","Where my task tools are ready"],minutes:[10,2,5],fallback:["Note next report step before answering","Correct one report line after the request","Choose the next feasible opening"],observation:["My output and next return move","Which switches were necessary","Which optional switch I deferred"]},suggested:{action:"Correct one report line; note next step",minimum:"Correct one report line",minutes:10,cueHint:"After today’s morning check-in",placeHint:"At my work desk",obstacleHint:"A routine request interrupts my task",fallbackHint:"Note next report step before answering",observationHint:"My output and next return move"},accessibleAlternative:"Use dictation or an accessible reminder; a two-minute useful task unit is valid.",stopRule:"Skip the marker for emergencies or immediate care; keep required contacts reachable."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do or produce?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"focus-1-decision",scenario:"Leila is checking report costs. A colleague needs a figure shortly. There is no emergency, and she can spare a brief note.",question:"Which response balances both tasks?",options:[{id:"focus-1-answer-now",text:"Answer the colleague immediately, then rely on memory to find the unfinished report step when she returns.",feedback:"This is responsive, but it discards an available low-cost return cue. A brief marker can name the next step before Leila handles the legitimate request."},{id:"focus-1-marker-first",text:"Note “check row 40 next,” handle the needed request, then return to that named report step.",feedback:"This respects the request and preserves a usable next move. The marker is a practical aid to try, not evidence that every interruption becomes harmless or recovery will be faster."},{id:"focus-1-finish-report",text:"Tell the colleague she will send the figure once the report is complete, then keep working without switching.",feedback:"Protecting the task matters, but the request is time-bound and a brief note is feasible. Finish everything first would ignore this dependency. Leave a marker, then respond."}],bestOptionId:"focus-1-marker-first"}},carry:{takeaway:"Protect useful work and leave a way back. Necessary interruptions still deserve a response.",followUp:{questionId:"focus-1-return",question:"Did your return marker tell you the next actual move?",observationPrompt:"What happened at the saved opportunity?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change fits your next opportunity?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Switch cost is a bounded finding",paragraphs:["Monsell's 2003 review describes experiments with simple cognitive tasks. Responses immediately after a switch were slower and usually more error-prone. Preparing in advance reduced the cost without reliably eliminating it. The review does not supply a pooled workplace-loss percentage or a universal recovery time. [F01] Use the distinction to choose which switches can wait, rather than declaring every interruption harmful.","Mark and colleagues' small 2008 simulated-email experiment illustrates another limit. Interrupted participants used less net task time while reporting more stress. Interruption-handling time was subtracted, so the finding does not mean faster total elapsed work. Quality measures were limited; it cannot guarantee unchanged real-world work quality. [F07] This lesson's short output-and-marker block is an ODA adaptation. Your task, role, health, and care responsibilities determine what is feasible. Count a useful line or task unit, not a timer running or a tally made while postponing the task."]},{heading:"What the return-plan evidence measured",paragraphs:["Leroy and Glomb's 2018 article reported four studies on interruptions and attention remaining with the original task. A return plan reduced that residue and supported decisions or recall on the interrupting task. The first field study was a survey of professionals, not an intervention experiment. The accessible abstract and university description do not establish a benefit on the original task after return. [F10]","The accessible abstract and university description do not supply a complete published worksheet or laboratory dose. A short note naming your stopping point and next action is therefore a source-informed ODA adaptation. Do not delay an emergency or immediate care need to complete it. At a routine pause, use words, dictation, or your usual accessible reminder. When you resume, check whether it named a usable action. An easier return is an observation, not a controlled test of the mechanism. Record what you produced and what still needs doing, even when the block ended early."]}],sourceIds:["F01","F07","F10"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"focus-2",courseId:"focus",locale:"en",title:"Every ping gets a vote. Choose yours",goal:"Keep needed contacts reachable and attempt your existing task with one fewer optional interruption.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:10,review:2},direction:{hook:"Three chat pings while you try to write a sentence? Choose what can wait for this block.",reason:{id:"focus-2-reason",label:"Who needs you now, and what can safely wait?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Care calls now; group chat later","Client deadline now; newsletters later","No required contact during this opening","Required work calls cannot wait"]},why:{id:"focus-2-purpose",label:"What would this move make possible in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Keep the same task and check who must reach you. One phone experiment changed alerts and visibility together. Students reported fewer attention-related symptoms in the lower-interruption week. It did not isolate notifications alone or show that the setup treats ADHD.","Use that boundary to design your own test. Preserve required contact channels first. Then change one optional interruption setting for a feasible block, keeping your previous phone placement. The work is the task unit you produce. Silencing an app is setup. Choose a message-check opportunity that fits your care and work responsibilities."],example:{title:"Leila chooses availability",text:"Leila keeps her report and ten-minute opening. Her father may need to call, so she checks that his channel stays available. She silences only the nonurgent group chat and keeps the phone where it was. She corrects another budget line. A work request still interrupts her; she records it and leaves a return marker."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your output. New plan? Edit this report example; preserve required contacts.",instructions:["Check required contact access before changing one optional interruption setting.","Name the output and real opening; settings alone are preparation."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Correct one report line with chat paused","Write one proposal sentence with chat paused","Solve one practice problem between required checks"],minimum:["Correct one report line; keep calls reachable","Write one proposal sentence; keep calls reachable","Complete one problem between required checks"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1},{label:"In two days",daysFromToday:2}],cue:["After today’s required message check","After lunch, before optional email","After my scheduled message check"],place:["At my work desk","At my kitchen table with phone reachable","At my usual work desk"],minutes:[10,2,5],fallback:["Keep care calls; pause only group chat","Restore access and choose another setting","Write one report sentence between required checks"],observation:["My output and required interruptions","Whether needed contacts could reach me","Which optional setting I changed"]},suggested:{action:"Correct one report line with chat paused",minimum:"Correct one report line; keep calls reachable",minutes:10,cueHint:"After today’s required message check",placeHint:"At my work desk",obstacleHint:"I am unsure whether a needed contact can reach me",fallbackHint:"Keep care calls; pause only group chat",observationHint:"My output and required interruptions"},accessibleAlternative:"Use a visible task card or accessible reminder; keep required calendars and communication tools.",stopRule:"Keep emergency, care, and required work channels available; do not trust untested exceptions."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do or produce?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"focus-2-decision",scenario:"Leila has a ten-minute report opening, a parent who may call, and repeated nonurgent group-chat pings. Contact exceptions are untested.",question:"What should she do first?",options:[{id:"focus-2-silence-all",text:"Use the phone’s default focus mode to reduce pings, keeping the device visible in case her parent calls.",feedback:"This reduces interruptions, but a visible phone does not verify its default focus-mode exceptions. Check that the needed care channel remains reachable before enabling settings that could block it."},{id:"focus-2-rely-on-restraint",text:"Leave every alert active and decide each time whether the message deserves an immediate reply.",feedback:"This preserves contact access, but leaves the repeated optional interruption in place. Leila can protect the group-chat boundary while keeping the care channel reachable."},{id:"focus-2-preserve-access",text:"Verify the care channel stays reachable, silence the nonurgent chat, then attempt the report and check messages afterward.",feedback:"This protects the task while respecting the actual care need. The setup is preparation. Record the report work separately, and revise any exception that did not work as intended."}],bestOptionId:"focus-2-preserve-access"}},carry:{takeaway:"Availability is a choice with responsibilities. Change optional interruptions while keeping needed access.",followUp:{questionId:"focus-2-return",question:"Did your setting protect the task while keeping needed contacts reachable?",observationPrompt:"What happened at the saved opportunity?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change fits your next opportunity?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"The experiment changed a bundle",paragraphs:["Kushlev and colleagues' 2016 counterbalanced experiment involved 221 university students. The high-interruption week combined enabled alerts with a phone within sight and reach. The lower-interruption week combined disabled auditory, vibration, and visual alerts with a phone kept out of sight. These changes happened together. Paired symptom comparisons used 190 respondents. [F06]","Average self-reported inattention was 2.38 versus 2.27 on a one-to-four scale, with reported d = .44. Hyperactivity ratings also differed modestly. Participants knew their settings, and this was one short student experiment. It did not isolate an alerts-only effect, establish ADHD causation or treatment, or demonstrate a significant direct productivity improvement. This lesson changes one optional setting while retaining your prior placement. That narrower personal trial is an ODA adaptation, not a reproduction of the week-long protocol. The reason to record output is to find out what actually happened to your chosen task."]},{heading:"A boundary has to fit the role",paragraphs:["Start with the real availability requirement. A care call, on-call channel, or time-bound work tool may need an immediate response. Do not assume that a named exception works without checking the setting. If you cannot confirm access, keep the channel available and adjust a different source of optional interruption. This is the course's safety and feasibility rule, not a promise from the phone study. [F06]","Use an external reminder or a visible next-step note if that makes the block accessible. Check messages afterward only when your role allows the delay. A blocked necessary message is a result that calls for correction, even if output improved. Persistent attention difficulties affecting daily functioning deserve professional assessment; settings are not treatment. The Cochrane review concerns structured cognitive-behavioral interventions for adults with ADHD, not this short routine. [F11] You can seek support now without proving that you failed enough phone experiments first. Keep the task, care, and record honest."]}],sourceIds:["F06","F11"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"focus-3",courseId:"focus",locale:"en",title:"Phone within reach? Make checking a choice",goal:"Test one workable phone placement during your task and record urges and pickups separately.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:10,review:2},direction:{hook:"Your phone on the desk is not a verdict on your discipline. Watch what you actually do.",reason:{id:"focus-3-reason",label:"What happens just before you reach for the phone?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["A sentence gets difficult","I expect a needed message","I check without noticing","No recent pickup to recall"]},why:{id:"focus-3-purpose",label:"What would this move make possible in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["A phone nearby and a phone you keep checking are different questions. Research reviews disagree about some memory tasks; one found no reliable overall impairment from mere presence. Neither conclusion settles what happens when you use the phone.","Keep your task, duration, and alerts similar across attempts. No record yet? Make this your first placement test. Change placement; preserve required access. Note both urges and actual pickups in this session and any comparison. Different workloads or fatigue limit the comparison. Use the record to choose a workable arrangement, not to prove your phone caused a result."],example:{title:"Leila compares matching observations",text:"Leila keeps her report, ten-minute block, and safe alert settings. She moves the phone from the desk to an accessible shelf. She records four urges, one pickup, and two corrected lines. Yesterday she recorded five urges and three pickups. Today was quieter, so she treats the shelf as worth trying again, not proven better."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your output. New plan? Edit this report example; preserve phone access.",instructions:["Change only placement; keep task, duration, alerts, and required access comparable.","Note urges and pickups separately after work if tallying distracts; mark uncertainty."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Correct one report line, phone on shelf","Correct one report line, phone on desk","Write one proposal sentence, phone safely reachable"],minimum:["Correct one report line","Write one usable report sentence","Complete one practice problem"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1},{label:"In two days",daysFromToday:2}],cue:["After today’s required message check","After confirming required phone access","After lunch, before optional email"],place:["At my desk, phone on reachable shelf","At my desk, phone safely accessible","At my kitchen table with phone reachable"],minutes:[10,2,5],fallback:["Stop tallying; note urges/pickups afterward, marking uncertainty","Use a reachable but less tempting spot","Stop counting and protect the task"],observation:["Urges, pickups, and actual task output","Task differences between the two blocks","Whether required access was preserved"]},suggested:{action:"Correct one report line, phone on shelf",minimum:"Correct one report line",minutes:10,cueHint:"After today’s required message check",placeHint:"At my desk, phone on reachable shelf",obstacleHint:"Counting events competes with doing the task",fallbackHint:"Stop live tallying; note remembered urges and pickups afterward. Mark uncertain counts as uncertain, not zero.",observationHint:"Urges, pickups, and actual task output"},accessibleAlternative:"Use an end-of-block note. Uncertain counts stay uncertain, not zero; missing counts do not erase actual task work.",stopRule:"Preserve required call and calendar access; choose another placement if access would be blocked."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do or produce?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"focus-3-decision",scenario:"Leila had fewer pickups on a quieter shelf day. She needs the phone for tomorrow’s task. Another ten-minute report opening allows the same measures.",question:"Which next plan answers tomorrow’s placement question?",options:[{id:"focus-3-provisional-placement",text:"Repeat the report and measures with the phone safely reachable, then choose a placement that fits this task.",feedback:"Tomorrow’s task needs phone access, so another comparable attempt addresses that constraint. The quieter shelf day supports a preference, not proof of what caused the pickup difference."},{id:"focus-3-settled-placement",text:"Keep the shelf for the week without further counting, using the quieter session as a practical preference.",feedback:"That can be a workable preference when access fits. Tomorrow’s task needs the phone, so retaining the shelf unchanged misses the new constraint. Fewer pickups also do not establish causation."},{id:"focus-3-ignore-observations",text:"Keep the phone on the desk because a review found no overall penalty from merely having it nearby.",feedback:"The desk may fit tomorrow’s phone need. But a pooled presence result does not answer Leila’s actual checking question. Try the reachable arrangement and comparable measures rather than discarding her observations."}],bestOptionId:"focus-3-provisional-placement"}},carry:{takeaway:"Compare the same events. Let observations guide a preference without turning them into a verdict.",followUp:{questionId:"focus-3-return",question:"Were your task, settings, urges, and pickups comparable enough to guide the next placement?",observationPrompt:"What happened at the saved opportunity?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change fits your next opportunity?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Why the headline is unsettled",paragraphs:["Hartanto and colleagues' 2024 synthesis pooled 33 studies, 53 samples, and 166 effects. It found no reliable overall impairment from a smartphone's mere presence: d = -.02, with a 95% confidence interval from -.06 to .01. A working-memory subgroup became nonsignificant after bias correction. The paper gives conflicting participant totals, so this course withholds a definitive total. [F14]","Parry's competing 2024 synthesis reported an adverse working-memory average while other cognitive summaries were null. Its accessible abstract identifies methodological differences and generally poor statistical power; the accessible abstract does not give the exact pooled effect. [F16] These reviews warrant uncertainty, not “phones never matter” or “a nearby phone always drains you.” Both examine presence rather than active checking. A desk can be a workable place for one task and an unhelpful temptation for another. Choose from role requirements and observations rather than guilt about the location alone."]},{heading:"A personal comparison can still be useful",paragraphs:["Ruiz Pardo and Minda’s 2022 preregistered direct replication did not reproduce the original location effect. The accessible primary abstract verifies that result without supplying a participant total or exact p value. A failed replication does not prove exact zero effects for every person, and it does not test active scrolling or alerts. [F13]","Your short trial answers a more practical question: where can you keep the phone while doing this task and preserving needed access? Count urges and pickups as different events, and record both in each session. Keep task, duration, and alert settings similar when feasible. Note unmatched demands rather than pretending life supplied laboratory controls. A new comparison scheduled for tomorrow is planned, not already tried. This one-variable approach is an ODA design choice, not a validated diagnostic test. Stop tallying if it displaces useful work. You can retain a workable preference while remaining unsure why it helped."]}],sourceIds:["F14","F16","F13"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"focus-4",courseId:"focus",locale:"en",title:"Rereading again? Take a pause you can return from",goal:"Take feasible rest, then return to one useful move in your existing task.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:3,review:2},direction:{hook:"Rereading the same line again? Your next move can include rest.",reason:{id:"focus-4-reason",label:"What does refusing a pause cost the rest of your day?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["I reread instead of deciding","I am less patient afterward","No observed cost yet","A pause is not currently feasible"]},why:{id:"focus-4-purpose",label:"What would this move make possible in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Short breaks improved average energy and fatigue in a research review, while the overall performance effect was unclear. Rest does not need to make you faster to matter. The review did not identify one best break length or activity.","Keep your task. If fatigue appeared, test rest; otherwise retain your workable plan. Choose a feasible pause and name the first useful move on return. Seated rest is valid. Keep required care access. Observe fatigue and whether you resumed; unchanged feelings are allowed. If you need more rest or support, change the plan instead of forcing the return."],example:{title:"Leila rests and resumes",text:"Leila has corrected budget rows for ten minutes and keeps rereading a note. She names her return move: explain the travel-cost change. She rests seated for two minutes, keeping care calls available, then writes one explanation sentence. She still feels tired. Her record says she paused and resumed, with no claim of faster work."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your return move. Need rest? Edit this seated-rest and report example.",instructions:["Name your actual return output before saving the editable example.","Rest, then try that move if it fits; record both honestly."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Rest seated, then explain one report change","Pause seated, then correct one budget line"],minimum:["Rest and write one report explanation","Rest and correct one budget line"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1},{label:"In two days",daysFromToday:2}],cue:["After my report-writing block","When I reread without making progress","At my agreed short pause"],place:["Seated at my work desk","In a nearby accessible rest place","Where required calls stay reachable"],minutes:[3,2,5],fallback:["Take needed rest and reschedule return","Use a shorter accessible pause","Request coverage for recovery time"],observation:["Whether I rested and resumed my move","Fatigue before and after the pause","The useful line on return"]},suggested:{action:"Rest seated, then explain one report change",minimum:"Rest and write one report explanation",minutes:3,cueHint:"After my report-writing block",placeHint:"Seated at my work desk",obstacleHint:"The planned return exceeds my available energy",fallbackHint:"Take needed rest and reschedule return",observationHint:"Whether I rested and resumed my move"},accessibleAlternative:"Use seated or nonmovement rest. Need rest alone? Choose Adapt this, then Rest.",stopRule:"Keep care access; if return is unsuitable, choose needed rest, support, or another opening."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do or produce?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"focus-4-decision",scenario:"Leila is drained after report work and has five minutes before a needed call. One explanation sentence remains manageable after seated rest.",question:"Which pause best fits this opening?",options:[{id:"focus-4-messages-break",text:"Use the five minutes to catch up on work messages, then return to whichever task feels most urgent.",feedback:"Messages may be necessary work, but they add new demands and leave the return undefined. This opening allows brief rest and a named report sentence before the call."},{id:"focus-4-rest-return",text:"Rest seated for two minutes, keep required calls available, then write the named explanation sentence if energy permits.",feedback:"This tests recovery while preserving a useful return and the needed call. An unchanged fatigue rating is valid. If energy does not permit the sentence, record that and revise."},{id:"focus-4-finish-before-rest",text:"Finish the explanation before the call so she can take a longer break afterward with nothing left open.",feedback:"Finishing first can be workable when energy permits. Here, Leila is already drained and brief rest fits. Try the pause before the manageable sentence rather than postponing recovery automatically."}],bestOptionId:"focus-4-rest-return"}},carry:{takeaway:"Rest is useful care. A return cue makes the next attempt clear without guaranteeing renewed energy.",followUp:{questionId:"focus-4-return",question:"Did the pause fit your needs, and was the named return move still feasible?",observationPrompt:"What happened at the saved opportunity?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change fits your next opportunity?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Recovery and output are different outcomes",paragraphs:["Albulescu and colleagues' 2022 review combined 22 samples and 2,335 participants, including students and workers. Breaks up to ten minutes improved vigor on average, d = .36 with a 95% confidence interval from .16 to .55, and fatigue, d = .35 from .19 to .50. Overall performance was uncertain, d = .16 from -.04 to .37. Outcome sample sizes differed. [F02]","The small and varied corpus does not establish an ideal duration, activity, or reliable performance benefit on demanding cognitive tasks. This lesson's two-minute seated pause and one useful line are accessible course choices, not the studied optimal dose. Screen-free rest may suit you; the review does not show that every screen-based leisure activity fails to restore energy. Record what you chose and noticed. Rest can serve patience, recovery, or presence in the life you want, even when output does not change. Do not make it conditional on finishing all work."]},{heading:"A schedule to try, not an interval to obey",paragraphs:["Biwer and colleagues compared fixed study-break schedules with self-chosen breaks in a two-hour student study session. The fixed schedules were 24 minutes of study with six minutes of rest, or 12 with three. There were 110 randomized students and 87 analyzed after missingness and exclusions. Some fatigue and concentration patterns favored fixed breaks, without a significant task-completion difference. The planned longer study was reduced to one session. [F08]","This does not prove a universal interval or long-term learning benefit. Use a cue that fits your role and an accessible form of rest. Name one return action, but change it when health, care, or energy requires more recovery. A timer is optional; an event cue can work as your plan. Fatigue ratings and the exact output-and-return record are ODA adaptations. They help you decide what to test next, without converting an unchanged feeling into failure or a required longer break into lost progress."]}],sourceIds:["F02","F08"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"focus-5",courseId:"focus",locale:"en",title:"Your real Tuesday needs a workable plan",goal:"Use your actual task records to choose next week's support and attempt one feasible block.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:10,review:2},direction:{hook:"A beautiful schedule is still a plan. Keep what helped you make actual progress this week.",reason:{id:"focus-5-reason",label:"What will you defer to protect a feasible block?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Nonurgent group chat","Extra formatting","An optional meeting follow-up","No safe deferral; choose another opening"]},why:{id:"focus-5-purpose",label:"What would this move make possible in your week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Look back at what you actually did on this task. Check output, required interruptions, and one support. Increasing progress monitoring improved goal attainment on average across experiments. It did not validate this exact routine. Your records guide a decision, not a causal verdict.","Keep supports that were workable. Change one condition your records question, and choose next week's realistic openings. Then do one useful task unit with the current plan. If no attempt exists, call this the first test. A future block stays planned. Neither a timer nor a course finish proves work happened."],example:{title:"Leila redraws the week",text:"Leila reads four report attempts. The chat boundary preserved care access, and she returned after two pauses. Mornings had fewer required work requests. She chooses two short morning openings next week. Today a necessary call leaves only five minutes, so she writes one useful explanation and records the smaller block. Her report still needs checking."}},move:{invitation:"Keep the act and opening. New plan? Edit this report example.",instructions:["Keep action, minimum, cue, and place; change one support only if needed.","Name the actual output and opening before saving this editable example.","Choose next week’s openings around obligations and recovery, then attempt the current move."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Write one report explanation with chat paused","Correct one budget line with calls reachable","Write one proposal sentence after a pause"],minimum:["Write one usable report explanation","Correct one budget line","Write one usable proposal sentence"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1},{label:"In two days",daysFromToday:2}],cue:["After today’s required morning check-in","After my required check-in","After lunch, before optional email"],place:["At my work desk","At my kitchen table with task tools","Where needed contacts stay reachable"],minutes:[10,2,5],fallback:["Write one report sentence before the call","Leave marker and handle required request","Take needed rest and choose another opening"],observation:["Actual output and one plan adjustment","Which support I kept or changed","Whether my plan fit my obligations"]},suggested:{action:"Write one report explanation with chat paused",minimum:"Write one usable report explanation",minutes:10,cueHint:"After today’s required morning check-in",placeHint:"At my work desk",obstacleHint:"A necessary obligation shortens my block",fallbackHint:"Write one report sentence before the call",observationHint:"Actual output and one plan adjustment"},accessibleAlternative:"Use accessible tools, assistance, or a shorter useful unit; external reminders remain valid.",stopRule:"Preserve required contacts and recovery; seek support for persistent impairment without waiting for course completion."},result:{question:"Did you do your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do or produce?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"focus-5-decision",scenario:"Leila planned ten minutes, but a needed call leaves five. One explanation sentence is feasible and her care access is intact.",question:"What adjustment uses her records well?",options:[{id:"focus-5-wait-full-block",text:"Save the report work for a full ten-minute opening tomorrow and use the remaining minutes for scheduling.",feedback:"Scheduling can protect another opening, but it is preparation. A useful sentence fits now. Attempt it, record the actual shorter block, and preserve the future plan as planned."},{id:"focus-5-extend-evening",text:"Move the full ten-minute block into already-short evening recovery so the original schedule remains complete today.",feedback:"A later block can sometimes fit, but this option sacrifices needed recovery to preserve the original duration. Use the feasible smaller unit instead and review the remaining work."},{id:"focus-5-smaller-output",text:"Write the feasible explanation sentence, record the shorter block, and redraw the next opportunity around actual obligations.",feedback:"This preserves useful output and respects the call and recovery. Record what actually happened rather than claiming ten minutes. Use that evidence to plan another feasible opening."}],bestOptionId:"focus-5-smaller-output"}},carry:{takeaway:"Keep the useful output, needed access, and recovery. Let your records redraw the next block.",followUp:{questionId:"focus-5-return",question:"What did your adjusted block produce?",observationPrompt:"What happened at the saved opportunity?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change fits your next opportunity?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"What your record can support",paragraphs:["Harkin and colleagues' 2016 review pooled 138 randomized studies with 19,951 participants overall. Interventions that increased monitoring improved goal attainment on average, d = .40 with a 95% confidence interval from .32 to .48. Goals and methods varied substantially. Associations with written or public reporting do not prove the superiority of this private focus record. [F03]","Use the record to distinguish a committed block, an actual attempt, its useful output, and a later review. A timer alone measures elapsed time. An unfinished report may still contain real completed lines; an attractive calendar contains no evidence of writing. Keep necessary and optional interruptions distinct. If the apparent best arrangement occurred on a quieter day, treat it as a preference to try again. Choose one change that responds to an observed problem rather than replacing every support at once. That sequence is an ODA design choice, not a tested treatment or causal analysis."]},{heading:"A schedule serves the task and the life",paragraphs:["Cal Newport describes time blocking as a personal scheduling framework, with room to redraw the day and blocks for reactive work. His reports of greater output are practitioner impressions, not measured intervention results. This author account supplies no trial of the routine. [F15] You do not need to schedule every working minute. Keep the cue, safe contact access, useful minimum, and recovery choices that served your week.","Choose a next-week review point and realistic opportunities, then test the next unit. If persistent attention difficulties impair daily life, seek professional assessment without waiting for course completion. That is a safety recommendation, not a finding from the monitoring studies. The Cochrane review concerns structured treatment for diagnosed adult ADHD; it supplies no diagnostic cutoff for this app. [F11] An honest pause or support request can finish learning. Only a reported actual task unit supplies evidence that task work happened."]}],sourceIds:["F03","F15","F11"]}}],a={id:"focus",title:"Busy all day? Give what matters your attention",subtitle:"Five lessons. Protect one useful task, keep needed contacts reachable, and test supports and recovery around real obligations.",description:"Choose a meaningful task and test interruption settings, phone placement, return markers, and rest around your actual life.",scope:"Suggestions for everyday working habits; this is not a diagnosis or treatment for attention disorders. If attention difficulties persist and affect your daily life, talk to a health professional. Do not silence calls you need for emergencies, caregiving or work.",outcome:"A feasible task experiment with useful output records, safe contact access, and a next-week work-and-rest plan.",photo:{id:"1505330622279-bf7d7fc918f4",alt:"A bright, tidy work desk"},lessons:[{id:"focus-1",v2:e[0],photo:{id:"1745474633597-be94033b16d6",alt:"A person working at a laptop, glancing at a phone"},schemaVersion:2,title:"Busy all day? Protect one useful move",minutes:6,goal:"Do a useful unit of your chosen task and leave a clear return marker.",reading:["Choose one task that serves your life, and keep it through this course. In laboratory studies, switching simple tasks slowed the next response and usually increased mistakes. Preparing reduced that cost without reliably removing it. This is not a universal productivity percentage.","For your next small block, produce a useful unit before an optional switch. If a routine interruption needs you, leave a short marker: where you stopped and the first move on return. That marker is a practical adaptation, not proven faster recovery. Skip it when immediate safety or care requires action."],practice:["For the next hour, make a tally mark every time you switch to another task or app.","Sort the switches: how many were your own choice, and how many came from a notification or another person?","Before your next switch, try a short preparation sentence: “Now I am moving on to …”"],reflection:"How did the switches feel to you?",question:"According to this lesson, what can working with interruptions cost you?",options:["It always makes you finish much more slowly.","Interruptions have no cost at all.","Even if your speed holds up, you may feel more stress and pressure."],correct:2,feedback:"In a small simulated-email experiment, interrupted participants used less net task time but reported more stress. Interruption-handling time was subtracted; this was not faster total elapsed work.",takeaway:"Protect useful work and leave a way back. Necessary interruptions still deserve a response.",sources:["F01","F07","F10"],deeper:[{heading:"Switch cost is a bounded finding",paragraphs:["Monsell's 2003 review describes experiments with simple cognitive tasks. Responses immediately after a switch were slower and usually more error-prone. Preparing in advance reduced the cost without reliably eliminating it. The review does not supply a pooled workplace-loss percentage or a universal recovery time. [F01] Use the distinction to choose which switches can wait, rather than declaring every interruption harmful.","Mark and colleagues' small 2008 simulated-email experiment illustrates another limit. Interrupted participants used less net task time while reporting more stress. Interruption-handling time was subtracted, so the finding does not mean faster total elapsed work. Quality measures were limited; it cannot guarantee unchanged real-world work quality. [F07] This lesson's short output-and-marker block is an ODA adaptation. Your task, role, health, and care responsibilities determine what is feasible. Count a useful line or task unit, not a timer running or a tally made while postponing the task."]},{heading:"What the return-plan evidence measured",paragraphs:["Leroy and Glomb's 2018 article reported four studies on interruptions and attention remaining with the original task. A return plan reduced that residue and supported decisions or recall on the interrupting task. The first field study was a survey of professionals, not an intervention experiment. The accessible abstract and university description do not establish a benefit on the original task after return. [F10]","The accessible abstract and university description do not supply a complete published worksheet or laboratory dose. A short note naming your stopping point and next action is therefore a source-informed ODA adaptation. Do not delay an emergency or immediate care need to complete it. At a routine pause, use words, dictation, or your usual accessible reminder. When you resume, check whether it named a usable action. An easier return is an observation, not a controlled test of the mechanism. Record what you produced and what still needs doing, even when the block ended early."]}],example:{title:"Leila leaves a return marker",text:"After the morning check-in, Leila works on the same budget report for ten minutes. She corrects one travel-cost line before opening email. A colleague needs a figure soon. She writes “Check row 40 next,” handles the request, and returns to that row. The report is unfinished; one correction and one return are real."}},{id:"focus-2",v2:e[1],photo:{id:"1526045612212-70caf35c14df",alt:"A person holding a smartphone"},schemaVersion:2,title:"Every ping gets a vote. Choose yours",minutes:6,goal:"Keep needed contacts reachable and attempt your existing task with one fewer optional interruption.",reading:["Keep the same task and check who must reach you. One phone experiment changed alerts and visibility together. Students reported fewer attention-related symptoms in the lower-interruption week. It did not isolate notifications alone or show that the setup treats ADHD.","Use that boundary to design your own test. Preserve required contact channels first. Then change one optional interruption setting for a feasible block, keeping your previous phone placement. The work is the task unit you produce. Silencing an app is setup. Choose a message-check opportunity that fits your care and work responsibilities."],practice:["Look at which apps have sent you notifications in the last hour.","Turn off or silence notifications for at least two apps that can safely wait.","Turn on “Do Not Disturb” for one work session and add exceptions for people who may need you urgently."],reflection:"Which notification was easy to turn off, and which was hard?",question:"Which is the most honest summary of this study’s result?",options:["The lower-interruption setup made a modest self-reported difference.","Notifications cause ADHD.","Turning notifications off solves all attention problems."],correct:0,feedback:"The study changed alerts and visibility together. Self-reported symptom ratings differed modestly; it did not isolate alerts alone or establish treatment or direct productivity gains.",takeaway:"Availability is a choice with responsibilities. Change optional interruptions while keeping needed access.",sources:["F06","F11"],deeper:[{heading:"The experiment changed a bundle",paragraphs:["Kushlev and colleagues' 2016 counterbalanced experiment involved 221 university students. The high-interruption week combined enabled alerts with a phone within sight and reach. The lower-interruption week combined disabled auditory, vibration, and visual alerts with a phone kept out of sight. These changes happened together. Paired symptom comparisons used 190 respondents. [F06]","Average self-reported inattention was 2.38 versus 2.27 on a one-to-four scale, with reported d = .44. Hyperactivity ratings also differed modestly. Participants knew their settings, and this was one short student experiment. It did not isolate an alerts-only effect, establish ADHD causation or treatment, or demonstrate a significant direct productivity improvement. This lesson changes one optional setting while retaining your prior placement. That narrower personal trial is an ODA adaptation, not a reproduction of the week-long protocol. The reason to record output is to find out what actually happened to your chosen task."]},{heading:"A boundary has to fit the role",paragraphs:["Start with the real availability requirement. A care call, on-call channel, or time-bound work tool may need an immediate response. Do not assume that a named exception works without checking the setting. If you cannot confirm access, keep the channel available and adjust a different source of optional interruption. This is the course's safety and feasibility rule, not a promise from the phone study. [F06]","Use an external reminder or a visible next-step note if that makes the block accessible. Check messages afterward only when your role allows the delay. A blocked necessary message is a result that calls for correction, even if output improved. Persistent attention difficulties affecting daily functioning deserve professional assessment; settings are not treatment. The Cochrane review concerns structured cognitive-behavioral interventions for adults with ADHD, not this short routine. [F11] You can seek support now without proving that you failed enough phone experiments first. Keep the task, care, and record honest."]}],example:{title:"Leila chooses availability",text:"Leila keeps her report and ten-minute opening. Her father may need to call, so she checks that his channel stays available. She silences only the nonurgent group chat and keeps the phone where it was. She corrects another budget line. A work request still interrupts her; she records it and leaves a return marker."}},{id:"focus-3",v2:e[2],photo:{id:"1635398235411-2c41ca32ffbc",alt:"A phone lying on a wooden table"},schemaVersion:2,title:"Phone within reach? Make checking a choice",minutes:6,goal:"Test one workable phone placement during your task and record urges and pickups separately.",reading:["A phone nearby and a phone you keep checking are different questions. Research reviews disagree about some memory tasks; one found no reliable overall impairment from mere presence. Neither conclusion settles what happens when you use the phone.","Keep your task, duration, and alerts similar across attempts. No record yet? Make this your first placement test. Change placement; preserve required access. Note both urges and actual pickups in this session and any comparison. Different workloads or fatigue limit the comparison. Use the record to choose a workable arrangement, not to prove your phone caused a result."],practice:["Notice where you usually keep your phone while you work and how many times you pick it up.","For one work session, put your phone somewhere you cannot reach, and note each time you feel the urge to pick it up.","On another day, try keeping the phone on the desk but silenced; compare the two days using your own observations."],reflection:"What is really hard for you: the phone being there, or the urge to pick it up?",question:"What does current evidence say about a phone simply sitting on the desk?",options:["It definitely drains your mind.","One review found no reliable overall impairment; memory summaries differ, and active use is separate.","Putting your phone in another room is pointless."],correct:1,feedback:"Mere-presence evidence is mixed across memory summaries. One synthesis found no reliable overall impairment. This does not settle active checking or individual preferences.",takeaway:"Compare the same events. Let observations guide a preference without turning them into a verdict.",sources:["F14","F16","F13"],deeper:[{heading:"Why the headline is unsettled",paragraphs:["Hartanto and colleagues' 2024 synthesis pooled 33 studies, 53 samples, and 166 effects. It found no reliable overall impairment from a smartphone's mere presence: d = -.02, with a 95% confidence interval from -.06 to .01. A working-memory subgroup became nonsignificant after bias correction. The paper gives conflicting participant totals, so this course withholds a definitive total. [F14]","Parry's competing 2024 synthesis reported an adverse working-memory average while other cognitive summaries were null. Its accessible abstract identifies methodological differences and generally poor statistical power; the accessible abstract does not give the exact pooled effect. [F16] These reviews warrant uncertainty, not “phones never matter” or “a nearby phone always drains you.” Both examine presence rather than active checking. A desk can be a workable place for one task and an unhelpful temptation for another. Choose from role requirements and observations rather than guilt about the location alone."]},{heading:"A personal comparison can still be useful",paragraphs:["Ruiz Pardo and Minda’s 2022 preregistered direct replication did not reproduce the original location effect. The accessible primary abstract verifies that result without supplying a participant total or exact p value. A failed replication does not prove exact zero effects for every person, and it does not test active scrolling or alerts. [F13]","Your short trial answers a more practical question: where can you keep the phone while doing this task and preserving needed access? Count urges and pickups as different events, and record both in each session. Keep task, duration, and alert settings similar when feasible. Note unmatched demands rather than pretending life supplied laboratory controls. A new comparison scheduled for tomorrow is planned, not already tried. This one-variable approach is an ODA design choice, not a validated diagnostic test. Stop tallying if it displaces useful work. You can retain a workable preference while remaining unsure why it helped."]}],example:{title:"Leila compares matching observations",text:"Leila keeps her report, ten-minute block, and safe alert settings. She moves the phone from the desk to an accessible shelf. She records four urges, one pickup, and two corrected lines. Yesterday she recorded five urges and three pickups. Today was quieter, so she treats the shelf as worth trying again, not proven better."}},{id:"focus-4",v2:e[3],photo:{id:"1556833232-52da3e4bd5d8",alt:"A woman walking along a path"},schemaVersion:2,title:"Rereading again? Take a pause you can return from",minutes:6,goal:"Take feasible rest, then return to one useful move in your existing task.",reading:["Short breaks improved average energy and fatigue in a research review, while the overall performance effect was unclear. Rest does not need to make you faster to matter. The review did not identify one best break length or activity.","Keep your task. If fatigue appeared, test rest; otherwise retain your workable plan. Choose a feasible pause and name the first useful move on return. Seated rest is valid. Keep required care access. Observe fatigue and whether you resumed; unchanged feelings are allowed. If you need more rest or support, change the plan instead of forcing the return."],practice:["Put a five- to ten-minute break at the end of one work session.","For the break, choose something that takes you away from the screen: getting up for a glass of water, looking out of the window or a short walk.","Before and after the break, rate how tired you feel from 1 to 5 and write it down."],reflection:"What did your break change for you, and what did it not change?",question:"Which statement about short breaks fits the evidence better?",options:["They definitely improve performance in every kind of work.","Taking a break is just a waste of time.","Promising for vigor and fatigue; the effect on performance is unclear."],correct:2,feedback:"The evidence is more consistent for feeling better than for performance. You can think of a break not as a tool for speeding up your work, but as part of looking after yourself.",takeaway:"Rest is useful care. A return cue makes the next attempt clear without guaranteeing renewed energy.",sources:["F02","F08"],deeper:[{heading:"Recovery and output are different outcomes",paragraphs:["Albulescu and colleagues' 2022 review combined 22 samples and 2,335 participants, including students and workers. Breaks up to ten minutes improved vigor on average, d = .36 with a 95% confidence interval from .16 to .55, and fatigue, d = .35 from .19 to .50. Overall performance was uncertain, d = .16 from -.04 to .37. Outcome sample sizes differed. [F02]","The small and varied corpus does not establish an ideal duration, activity, or reliable performance benefit on demanding cognitive tasks. This lesson's two-minute seated pause and one useful line are accessible course choices, not the studied optimal dose. Screen-free rest may suit you; the review does not show that every screen-based leisure activity fails to restore energy. Record what you chose and noticed. Rest can serve patience, recovery, or presence in the life you want, even when output does not change. Do not make it conditional on finishing all work."]},{heading:"A schedule to try, not an interval to obey",paragraphs:["Biwer and colleagues compared fixed study-break schedules with self-chosen breaks in a two-hour student study session. The fixed schedules were 24 minutes of study with six minutes of rest, or 12 with three. There were 110 randomized students and 87 analyzed after missingness and exclusions. Some fatigue and concentration patterns favored fixed breaks, without a significant task-completion difference. The planned longer study was reduced to one session. [F08]","This does not prove a universal interval or long-term learning benefit. Use a cue that fits your role and an accessible form of rest. Name one return action, but change it when health, care, or energy requires more recovery. A timer is optional; an event cue can work as your plan. Fatigue ratings and the exact output-and-return record are ODA adaptations. They help you decide what to test next, without converting an unchanged feeling into failure or a required longer break into lost progress."]}],example:{title:"Leila rests and resumes",text:"Leila has corrected budget rows for ten minutes and keeps rereading a note. She names her return move: explain the travel-cost change. She rests seated for two minutes, keeping care calls available, then writes one explanation sentence. She still feels tired. Her record says she paused and resumed, with no claim of faster work."}},{id:"focus-5",v2:e[4],photo:{id:"1632772998001-cc9bf6f7c852",alt:"A planner with two pens lying on it"},schemaVersion:2,title:"Your real Tuesday needs a workable plan",minutes:6,goal:"Use your actual task records to choose next week's support and attempt one feasible block.",reading:["Look back at what you actually did on this task. Check output, required interruptions, and one support. Increasing progress monitoring improved goal attainment on average across experiments. It did not validate this exact routine. Your records guide a decision, not a causal verdict.","Keep supports that were workable. Change one condition your records question, and choose next week's realistic openings. Then do one useful task unit with the current plan. If no attempt exists, call this the first test. A future block stays planned. Neither a timer nor a course finish proves work happened."],practice:["Choose one task for tomorrow and a block length that suits you; starting short is fine.","Complete the sentence “If my attention drifts, then I will …” and set up your notifications before the block starts.","After the block and the break, briefly note what worked and what you would change."],reflection:"Which part of your focus plan supports you the most?",question:"What is the most honest way to approach time blocking?",options:["It is the best method for everyone, and it is proven.","A reasonable personal experiment; you look at the result and adjust.","If a block falls apart, the whole day is wasted."],correct:1,feedback:"Time blocking is a scheduling option to test. The author account supplies no intervention trial of this specific routine. Future planning is not an attempted block, and persistent impairment deserves professional support.",takeaway:"Keep the useful output, needed access, and recovery. Let your records redraw the next block.",sources:["F03","F15","F11"],deeper:[{heading:"What your record can support",paragraphs:["Harkin and colleagues' 2016 review pooled 138 randomized studies with 19,951 participants overall. Interventions that increased monitoring improved goal attainment on average, d = .40 with a 95% confidence interval from .32 to .48. Goals and methods varied substantially. Associations with written or public reporting do not prove the superiority of this private focus record. [F03]","Use the record to distinguish a committed block, an actual attempt, its useful output, and a later review. A timer alone measures elapsed time. An unfinished report may still contain real completed lines; an attractive calendar contains no evidence of writing. Keep necessary and optional interruptions distinct. If the apparent best arrangement occurred on a quieter day, treat it as a preference to try again. Choose one change that responds to an observed problem rather than replacing every support at once. That sequence is an ODA design choice, not a tested treatment or causal analysis."]},{heading:"A schedule serves the task and the life",paragraphs:["Cal Newport describes time blocking as a personal scheduling framework, with room to redraw the day and blocks for reactive work. His reports of greater output are practitioner impressions, not measured intervention results. This author account supplies no trial of the routine. [F15] You do not need to schedule every working minute. Keep the cue, safe contact access, useful minimum, and recovery choices that served your week.","Choose a next-week review point and realistic opportunities, then test the next unit. If persistent attention difficulties impair daily life, seek professional assessment without waiting for course completion. That is a safety recommendation, not a finding from the monitoring studies. The Cochrane review concerns structured treatment for diagnosed adult ADHD; it supplies no diagnostic cutoff for this app. [F11] An honest pause or support request can finish learning. Only a reported actual task unit supplies evidence that task work happened."]}],example:{title:"Leila redraws the week",text:"Leila reads four report attempts. The chat boundary preserved care access, and she returned after two pauses. Mornings had fewer required work requests. She chooses two short morning openings next week. Today a necessary call leaves only five minutes, so she writes one useful explanation and records the smaller block. Her report still needs checking."}}]};export{a as COURSE,e as FOCUS_LESSONS_V2,t as SOURCES};
